+The Strict profile grants `/dev/tty*` read, write and ioctl, global `file-read-metadata`, and `/Applications`, `/etc` and `/private/etc` reads by default (unchanged; a PTY child needs its tty). Three opt-in knobs tighten it: `deny_tty(true)` denies terminal devices (and drops the `WriteOnly` tty write), `scope_metadata(true)` limits metadata to the granted and system roots and the path walk to them, and `minimal_system_reads(true)` drops `/Applications`, `/etc` and `/private/etc`. Unverified on a Mac.
+
 ## Secrets and denial reports
 
 `SandboxPolicy::deny_read(path)` and `deny_read_glob(pattern)` deny reads and writes of a path or absolute glob inside a granted root. `secret_paths_within(root)` lists the protected folders (`~/.ssh`, `.aws`, `.gnupg`, `.kube`, `.docker`, `.config`, `.npm`, ...) beneath a root, `plan_deny_read_paths` plans a deny set (lexical and canonical spellings; relative paths, `..` and filesystem roots, including an alias that resolves to one, are refused), and `path_is_protected_secret(path)` answers for one path, resolving `..` and symlinks. `report_denial(output, command, exit_ok, &ConfinementInfo)` returns a `DenialReport`; its `requested_grant` is a request for a person or an admission authority, never an approval: output is untrusted, so a protected secret, a filesystem root or a `..` path never gets one.
